{"input": "", "answers": ["A day after being accidentally introduced as \"President Clinton,\" Hillary seems to be taking pains to kill talk of a future run in New Zealand's media, reports Reuters. Asked whether she'd \"ruled out\" running through 2016, she replied, \"Oh yes, yes. I'm very pleased to be doing what I'm doing as secretary of state.\" Asked in another interview whether she could be the first female president, she answered, \"Well, not me. But it will be someone ...\" The significance? Probably not much, writes Laura Rozen in Politico, noting how one of Clinton's answers is tied to her being secretary of state—a post she likely won't hold in 2016. On another front, Clinton said the midterm losses were \"big—but not out of the pattern of historical political elections.\" She added, \"I think the president made very clear was he made decisions which were essential for the well-being of the American people.\""], "length": 1038, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton ruled out running for president in 2012 or 2016, saying the United States should be ready for a woman president. Clinton made the comments in interviews in New Zealand, where she is on a two-week Asia-Pacific tour that wraps up in Australia. Clinton said she has no plans to run again despite talk fueled by Democrats' losses in Tuesday's U.S. elections. She defended President Barack Obama and put the Democrats' losses in perspective, saying it was not a bigger rout than her husband experienced in 1994. Clinton said Obama made decisions that were essential for the well-being of the American people, even if they were unpopular. She said the party needs to redouble its efforts to win back voters. Clinton also used the opportunity to praise New Zealand for electing women to high-ranking positions. She said it was nice to come to a country that has already proven they can elect women to the highest governing positions in their systems. Clinton's comments came as Democrats lost control of the House of Representatives and Republicans gained at least 60 seats. The Senate remains in Democratic control with a 51-seat majority, but Republicans gained six seats there as well. Clinton's comments were seen as an attempt to defuse speculation about her running for president again. She has been mentioned as a possible candidate in 2016, but she has not made any official announcement."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Amy Schumer took the long way around to announce she's pregnant with husband Chris Fischer. The comedian and actress broke her baby news Monday on the Instagram stories of friend and journalist Jessica Yellin, the AP reports. Yellin, of the site NewsNotNoise.org, showed at the end of a list of Schumer's recommended congressional and gubernatorial candidates the line: \"I'm pregnant-Amy Schumer.\" Schumer had teased the announcement on her own Instagram page, captioning a photo of hers and Fischer's heads atop Prince Harry's and Meghan Markle's bodies, \"About to announce some exciting news on @jessicayellin insta page. Please follow her for up to the minute #newsnotnoise she breaks down what’s really going on. She agreed to post a lil noise today for me! Follow her and VOTE!!\" \"We are 15 days out from the midterms and there is a lot going on,\" Yellin explained before going into Schumer's \"lengthy\" list of recommendations, per Bustle. \"I wanted to share some news from our community. Maybe it's noise, but it's happy noise,\" Yellin continued. \"These are the recommendations of Amy Schumer, one of the most consistent and early supporters of #NewsNotNoise. Now, read all the way to the bottom. You'll see there's some news down there. Congratulations, Amy.\" Schumer is known for her liberal politics: She was recently arrested protesting the confirmation of Judge Brett Kavanaugh to the US Supreme Court. The 37-year-old married Fischer, a chef, in February. (Here's why Schumer won't appear in any Super Bowl ads.)"], "length": 1133, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Amy Schumer is not planning to appear in any Super Bowl commercials this year. She is sitting out in solidarity with Colin Kaepernick and his take-a-knee protest against racism and police brutality. Schumer explained her decision at length on Instagram, challenging white NFL players to kneel as well. She also announced that she will not be doing a Super Bowl commercial this year, saying that 'opposing the NFL is like opposing the NRA. Both are very tough.' She received support from Jessica Seinfeld, Diddy, and Christie Brinkley among others. In 2016, Schumer appeared in a political campaign for Bud Light with Rogen. She has been a vocal advocate for gun control and other causes she cares about. She recently spoke out in support of the transgender community and Colin Kaepernick's peaceful protest of the NFL. Schumer is expecting her first child and used her baby announcement to remind people to vote in the upcoming midterm elections."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Chicken giant Tyson Foods says it will retrain all of its workers who deal with live birds after an animal rights group released hidden footage from within its facilities on Wednesday, USA Today reports. According to the Washington Post, the video from Compassion Over Killing shows workers choking, punching, and kicking chickens, plus running them over with forklifts and leaving them in piles to die. One worker in the video says it's \"inhumane\" to kill a chicken by stepping on its head while he kills a chicken by stepping on its head. Tyson calls the behavior \"inexcusable\" and says it fired 10 employees at the Virginia facilities shown in the video. Animal rights activists have infiltrated Tyson Foods to film such videos at least five times in a little over a year."], "length": 1671, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Compassion Over Killing, an animal rights organization, released a video showing mistreatment of chickens by Tyson Foods employees. The video was taken by an undercover investigator who worked at Tyson's processing facilities. The footage shows Tyson employees throwing, kicking, and punching chickens, as well as sticking plastic rods through their beaks in a process called \"boning.\" The organization condemned the behavior and called for Tyson to be held accountable. Tyson fired 10 employees who were depicted in the video and discontinued the practice of \"boning.\" The company also announced that it would retrain all of its live poultry workers on animal welfare policies. The mistreatment of chickens comes after Perdue, another chicken processor, announced plans to kill chickens using carbon dioxide or argon gas. Compassion Over Killing called for a ban on the practice of raising and killing animals for food. Tyson said it would take further steps to ensure animal welfare procedures are followed throughout its operations."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Donald Trump's lawyer has apparently decided that it's his turn to make the gaffes. In an interview with CNN Wednesday, notoriously combative Trump Organization attorney Michael Cohen interrupted host Brianna Keilar when she said that the campaign was \"down,\" Politico reports. \"Says who?\" he asked. \"Polls. Most of them. All of them?\" she replied. \"Says who?\" Cohen asked again. When she repeated it was polls, he responded, \"OK, which polls?” and Keilar said: “All of them.\" USA Today reports that Twitter users were quick to mock Cohen and are now replying \"Says who?\" to all his tweets. Cohen, in what Law Newz calls \"typical lawyer fashion,\" also took issue with Trump's overhaul of campaign staff being called a \"shake-up,\" insisting it was actually a \"change-up.\" Cohen also raised eyebrows when he told Kielar that Trump knows about \"the African-American problem in this country,\" the Independent reports. \"What I meant to say is the problem that exists in the African-American community,\" he later said. (Michael Moore says he \"knows for a fact\" that Trump never really wanted to be president.)"], "length": 1533, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Donald Trump's campaign has hired two new officials to oversee its struggling campaign. Michael Cohen, Trump's lawyer, was asked about the campaign's low ratings in polls. He responded by saying \"Says who?\" when asked if the campaign was down. Cohen also said that Trump was \"colourblind\" but aware of the racial tensions in the US. The exchange was awkward and did not reassure voters that the campaign was back on track. Trump's campaign has also hired Breitbart News executive chairman Steve Bannon as its chief executive and Kellyanne Conway as campaign manager. The shake-up comes after Trump fired Corey Lewandowski, who is now a political commentator. The campaign has also hired Roger Ailes, the former head of Fox News, as an adviser. Trump's campaign is struggling in swing states such as Virginia and New Hampshire. The campaign is also facing a challenge from Hillary Clinton, who is leading in polls. Trump's running mate, Mike Pence, is the governor of Indiana, which generally votes Republican. However, Trump won the Iowa state fair corn poll. The exchange between Cohen and Keilar sparked a Twitter trend, with people responding to Cohen's \"Says who?\" with \"The polls. Most of them. All of them.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The school resource officer suspended after videos of him throwing a black female high-school student around a South Carolina classroom went viral Monday has been fired, CBS News reports. Richland County Sheriff Leon Lott announced the firing of Ben Fields Wednesday after an investigation found his \"use of force was unacceptable\" and he didn't follow procedure during the arrest. According to NBC News, the decision was made after the sheriff's department looked at videos of the incident and interviewed witnesses. \"From the very beginning that's what's caused me to be upset, and continued to upset me, is that he picked the student up and threw the student across the room,\" Lott says. The Department of Justice is also conducting its own investigation into the incident. This isn't the first time Fields, who is white, has faced accusations of excessive force and racial bias. He's been involved in three other such cases dating back to 2005, and he's scheduled to go to trial over a 2013 racial bias incident in January."], "length": 2227, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A South Carolina sheriff's deputy has been fired after a video showed him forcefully removing a student from a classroom. The deputy, Senior Ben Fields, was suspended before the incident and has been the subject of racial bias and excessive force allegations. The incident occurred when a student became disruptive in her algebra class and refused to leave. Fields was called to the classroom and body-slammed the student to the ground. The student was arrested along with a second student who was also contributing to the chaos. The FBI and Justice Department are investigating the incident to determine whether the girl's civil rights were violated. The sheriff has apologized for Fields' actions and said he will not release his personnel file. The school district has blasted Fields' actions as \"outrageous\" and \"reprehensible\" and is strengthening its training efforts with school resource officers."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The man who murdered John Lennon will remain behind bars after his 10th unsuccessful parole hearing, reports the Daily News. Mark David Chapman, now 63, was turned down again this week. No reasons have yet been made public, but the newspaper reports that Yoko Ono sent the parole board a letter asking that Chapman remain in the Wende Correctional Facility in upstate New York—not only for the safety of her and her family, but for his own safety. Chapman, who shot Lennon in New York City in 1980, is up again for parole in about two years. Chapman's wife recently told the UK Mirror that he talked of killing Lennon two months before actually doing so."], "length": 1567, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Gloria Chapman, the wife of John Lennon assassin Mark David Chapman, has revealed that her husband told her he was going to murder Lennon two months before he shot the Beatle dead on his New York doorstep. She said he never carried out the threat at the time because of his love for her and dumped the gun he had used. Gloria said she knew it was Chapman who was the assassin as soon as news came through that Lennon had been shot. She has now been waiting for nearly four decades behind bars for her husband to be freed after he was given 20 to life to years to life to murder Lennon on December 8, 1980. Gloria, 67, said it didn’t matter how long Mark was in prison. I would wait for him. She has had the chance to have sex with him during prison visits in a caravan on the grounds. Gloria was 5,000 miles away in Hawaii at the time the devastating news came in that the 40-year-old was dead. She said: I knew it was Mark. How did I know? Two months earlier, Mark had travelled to New York. He came back home, scared, telling me that to make a name for himself he needed to kill Lennon. But my love saved him. She added: He threw the gun into the ocean, and I believed him. But he lied to me. Now she hopes, despite objections from Lennon’s widow Yoko Ono, that her wait to have him home is a few weeks from ending."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["See, this is why all those Twitter profiles are sure to note that retweets do not equal endorsements. CBS News reports the Pentagon's official Twitter account on Thursday retweeted a tweet calling for President Trump's resignation. \"GOP: Stop making sexual assault a partisan issue,\" tweeted \"Proud Resister\" while calling for Roy Moore, Sen. Al Franken, and Trump to all resign or step aside due to allegations of sexual misconduct. The tweet was retweeted—and then soon deleted—by the Department of Defense Twitter account. According to Politico, Pentagon spokesperson Col. Rob Manning confirmed the retweet was made by the \"authorized operator\" of the department's Twitter account. In her own tweet, Pentagon spokesperson Dana White said the call for Trump's resignation was \"erroneously retweeted\" and contained \"content that would not be endorsed by the Department of Defense.\""], "length": 707, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The Pentagon's official Twitter account retweeted a call for President Trump to resign, which was quickly deleted. The retweet was made \"erroneously\" by an authorized operator of the account, according to the Department of Defense's chief spokesperson. The tweet was from the account \"Proud Resister,\" which links to a website selling anti-Trump collectibles. The retweet was deleted immediately after it was posted. The Department of Defense has clarified that the retweet was not endorsed by the Department of Defense. The incident has raised questions about the president's own behavior in light of sexual harassment allegations against other figures in Washington and Hollywood."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Peter Jackson's crafts a fantastic vision of the afterlife in The Lovely Bones, say critics, but some feel it came at the expense of the human side of the story of a murdered teen, adapted from the book by Alice Sebold. \"By turns warmly sentimental, serial-killer sinister, and science-fiction fantastical, The Lovely Bones was an unlikely book to achieve worldwide success,\" Kenneth Turan writes in the Los Angeles Times. \"Those mismatched elements come back to haunt\" the film version, \"making the final product more hit-and-miss than unblemished triumph.\" \"Sebold's Lovely Bones is fleshed out with the perilous, irresistible power of sex—a real world of extramarital sex and sex between young lovers in addition to the heinous rape from which moviegoers are shielded.\" But Jackson \"shies from the challenge, shortchanging a story that isn't only about the lightness of souls in heaven but also about the urges of bodies on earth,\" writes Lisa Schwarzbaum at Entertainment Weekly. \"Through Jackson's art\" and the magic of star Saoirse Rogan, Richard Corliss writes at Time, \"the obscenity of child murder has been invested with immense gravity and grace.\" \"Some books are not meant to be adapted to the big screen,\" and The Lovely Bones is one of them, writes Claudia Puig at USA Today. What works in the book, she writes, comes off as \"artificial and emotionless on-screen.\""], "length": 3045, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The movie adaptation of Alice Sebold's bestselling novel \"The Lovely Bones\" was released in 2009. The film was directed by Peter Jackson and starred Saoirse Ronan, Stanley Tucci, Mark Wahlberg, and Rachel Weisz. The movie follows the story of a 14-year-old girl named Susie who is murdered by her neighbor and left watching her family from the \"in-between.\" The film received mixed reviews from critics, with some praising the performances of the cast and the visual effects, while others found the tone of the movie to be inconsistent and the depiction of heaven to be cheesy. The movie was rated PG-13 for mature thematic material involving disturbing violent content and images, and some language. It was released nationwide on Friday, December 11, 2009."}
