{"input": "", "answers": ["Connor Cummins' family describes him to WABC-TV as a photography devotee—and that enthusiasm may have cost him his life. The New Jersey man, said to be either 20 or 24 years old, and an 18-year-old pal ascended the scaffolding outside NYC's Four Seasons hotel Wednesday night in search of the perfect panoramic picture—but Cummins may have slipped on railings made slippery by the rain and fell nine stories to his death, cops tell NJ.com. An NYPD spokesman tells the New York Daily News that the initial investigation suggests the men weren't guests of the hotel. Cummins, who originally hails from Rockaway and whose Facebook page indicates he's now a student at the University of Massachusetts Amherst, per ABC, landed on one of the hotel's lower-level rooftops around 11:15pm and was pronounced dead at the scene, reports the New York Post. His friend wasn't injured and was questioned by authorities to fill in the blanks; he was released Thursday morning without being charged. \"He was a loving boy, that's all I can say,\" one of Cummins' relatives tells the Post. \"I don't know what happened.\" (There's more suspicion surrounding a Muslim teen's fatal fall off a Seattle building.)"], "length": 1299, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A 20-year-old man from Rockaway, New Jersey, fell to his death while trying to take pictures from the scaffolding of the 52-story Four Seasons Hotel in Manhattan. Connor Cummings was pronounced dead at the scene. Cummings was a sophomore at the University of Massachusetts at Amherst and was visiting New York City. He was not a guest at the hotel. Cummings and his friend, Dimitri Oliveras, were climbing scaffolding in an interior shaft of the hotel when Cummings fell. Oliveras climbed down the ladder and fled the scene. Cummings was a photography buff and was taking pictures of the city. He was pronounced dead at the scene. Cummings was a member of the fraternity Alpha Sigma Phi and was known for his love of photograph